I've been playing Nethack since I was like 15.  That was in 1995 or so,
on my 486/33; having logged at least 20000+ games on it (counted in the
log file) since, the RNG finally decided to 'give me an easy one' and
pretty much let me cruise through the game with no problem.  I'm sure I
deserved every death I got.

Had been goofing around lately with Tourists and Rangers without much
luck; the Rangers would always muddle around for awhile without being
able to catch up to the increasing difficulty of the monsters, and the
Tourists would sometimes make it surprisingly far (one had survived
until the Castle; after generating a bit of a superiority complex with
his assembled ascension kit he had removed his armor to frolic with a
succubus, and forgot to put it back on before a black dragon slapped
him with a DYWYPI? with a disintegration blast. Lesson learned.)  So I
rolled up a female priestess with marginal stats and went to work.

Wishes = win.  The RNG started throwing useful crap at me at nearly
every opportunity.  A few early magic lamps gave me +2 blessed grey
dragon scale mail (really hemmed and hawed over this one) and some
other goods.  Sokoban accordingly ponied up an amulet of reflection and
with the magic marker I found in the minetown shop(!), I was in
business.  Used my second wish to get 2 blessed scrolls of charging and
had found 3 magic markers in relatively short order.  My AC was -28
before I knew it.

Found a bones pile right inside minetown, with a coaligned altar, too.
Mad loot and free money.  Priests are great with bones piles - half of
the stuff isn't cursed.  After cleaning out the shops (I don't think I
even bothered with Izchak's candles), I was set.

Threw a barbecue, and Chupacabra or whatever the neutral god's name is
brought Magicbane to the party.  Unf.  While it wasn't the beefiest
weapon in town, it still hit fairly hard once I popped it to +6.  It
did, however, make the elemental planes hard (more on that later).

Finished the quest level before even going anywhere near Medusa's lair.
 Finally, FINALLY decided to abuse cursed genocide for Wraiths and went
nuts with it.  I was level 19 or so before even getting to the quest
lair (needless to say, I qualified).  The quest level also coughed up a
number of wraith corpses and I was stacking up the hit points.  Also
cursed genocided for nurses at some point and stroked the hitpoint
action.  Was at about 200HP by this point, and more MP than I knew what
to do with.  The Mitre of Holiness is a pretty scandalously powerful
item, but it's super rad when you blow a wish on the Eyes of the
Aethiopica as well (guilty!).  Not even the Amulet of Yendor could drag
this freight train down.

Put on my boots of water walking -and- a ring of levitation (can never
be too certain) and cruised across the water to Medusa's island and
broke in.  Punched her twice with Magicbane and she popped like a
grape.

Interestingly, I never actually found any pair of gloves (at all) until
the castle level, when the soldiers dropped them in spades, so
cockatrice smiting was out of the question (I usually give them a wide
berth anyway).  I left a fat stash of food rations near the staircase
here since they were weighing me down, but I didn't even grab them on
the way up.

The castle wishes were prodiguous.  Not only did I loot the 0:3 wand of
wishing (which stayed with me until the endgame), but the castle throne
conceded not one, but two(!) wishes to go with another throne wish
earlier in the game.  I got the speed boots I'd always wanted, got the
Eyes in one wish, got a few magic markers for enchantment abusing.. and
crap, a t-shirt.  I think my AC hit like -45 at one point.  (YANI: I
think someone mentioned it, but you should be able to write messages on
your T-shirt with a magic marker.)

Weird message - while sacrificing, I got the message that my god had
granted me telepathy, 'use it wisely!'  Thanks, Chupacabra, but I
already had telepathy, I ate a floating eye.  What's the difference?

Both Vlad and Juiblex were stupendously easy.  The wand of digging
trick with Juiblex is the only way I've ever had to deal with him.
Vlad died to something like 2 magic missiles.  Never buggered with
Ludios.

I'd found a spellbook of identify, which made the rest of the game
super easy, and wished up a spellbook of magic mapping, which made
Gehennom super easy.  Along with the spellbook of dig I found
somewhere, I didn't even have to lug around a pickaxe to clear my
escape route through the underworld - and with liberal application of
the Eyes and the Mitre, I never ever ever needed mana for more than 100
turns.  I kept a relatively slow pace going downstairs so that I
wouldn't be surprised.

Orcus gave me trouble - not the 'I'm going to die' trouble, but the
'why won't you freaking die?' trouble.  Yeenoghu showed up for the
ride, as well, and Orcus exhausted all of the charges in his stupid
wand of death bouncing them off of my grey dragon scale mail while
Yeenoghu flailed helplessly with his.. flail.  Their combined
devastation was something in the area of 3-5 hit points per turn,
enough to make me yawn - but they regularly summoned beasties, and
besides, he had my wand!  I eventually just sat on the stairs and beat
them to death with Magicbane, and got an empty boomstick for my
troubles.  Shouldn't have dawdled.

Couldn't exactly remember how to get into the Wizard's tower, but
stumbled upon it after goofing around for a little while (didn't feel
like going to the gazetteer.  Oh, by the way, at some point I killed
Vlad.  He's too easy.)  Bapped the Wizard with .. something like 3 or 4
magic missiles.  Anyway, he went up in smoke and I grabbed the Book.

Combed level 50 for the vibrating square, blah blah blah. Finally found
a ring of conflict somewhere down here, and found a priest of Moloch
who took all the rest of my money off my hands on the way back up.
Lots of fistfighting in the Valley of the Dead, although liberal
application of magic missiles made the trip faster than usual.  Most of
the monsters took care of themselves, including a few bone devils that
whacked some of the priests of Moloch at the bottom floor.  That was
amusing.

Moloch's Sanctum - of course, the RNG rolls sevens again and a
cockatrice dies right at the entrance.  I finally give into the
temptation and pick that mofo up.  *bap*  Statue.  *zot*  Rubble.  Hey,
what's this?  *Swipe*

The monsters are concentrating on fighting each other and I make it out
of Gehennom mostly unscathed, although the mysterious force compensates
for the brutally efficient run I've had so far by sending me back no
less than 6 times (and I fell down a hole once, oops).  The Wizard
didn't bother me much, and I'd been carting around a cockatrice corpse
for good measure.  I had the foresight to blast him with the corpse,
and break the statue for kicks, but I didn't think to hit the rocks
with a stone to flesh spell.  'This Wizard meatball is delicious!'
Would have made an interesting souvenier

This was my only near YASD - I thoughtlessly floated over a sink, with
the usual consequences.  I was packing the mandatory Amulet of Life
Saving for the ADHD crowd, and I made sure to get another one out of my
overflowing Bag of Holding before taking another (floating) step.

One last equipment check - everything in place?  No need to burn a wish
for the Eyes of the Overworld.  (Should have.)

Earth is super easy.  I'm already equipped for the ride, and close
quarters means I make mincemeat of the Wizard.  Earth elementals are a
walk in the park and I stumble upon the portal before I know it.

Air was the plane that gave me trouble last time I got this far, but
this time no Archons spawned and the Ds and Es weren't hard-hitting
enough to cause any real problems.  A few zots of the slow monster wand
and I was home free.  Used a gold detection scroll to find the portal,
saving myself potentially hours.

Fire was the real problem this time, as the Archons that were late to
the party in Air came to Fire instead.  Magicbane isn't a real
beatstick of a weapon, and Archons (AFAIK) are immune to practically
every magic spell under the sun.  They'd only be marginally tough if
they didn't summon hordes of beasties, but sure enough, I couldn't kill
them fast enough and we're surrounded by fire elementals, dragons, and
nasties of all sorts - and they're actually starting to chew through my
HP.  Fortunately, I remember a trick from reading the forums here, and
break a wand of teleportation over my head; sure enough, it creates a
hole and I run through it like my name was Shaun Alexander.  What a
bonus!  Two thumbs up for that one, guys- saved my bacon.  Another
scroll of gold detection goes poof and I'm ready to cool down with a
dip in the Water.

I'm trying to parse what exactly the representation is in the plane of
water - are we floating above an ocean?  I thought it'd be a solid mass
of water like in AD&D.  Anyway, the Es are helpless and I quickly dive
into the portal (thanks, gold detection!)  No problem here.

And here we are, the Astral plane - I have to visit all three rooms.  I
dispatch Chupacabra's angels quickly (no need for them) and start
plowing through the mess.  I hit up all three Horsemen IIRC, and was
surprised at how weak they were.  66 hit points?  I eat kids like you
for breakfast.  I melee Famine and Death with Magicbane.  No worries.

I used my last handy wish to get an extra wand of death here, although
I don't have to use it much.  I blaze through a few funny situations,
such as the priests of Moloch killing Pestilence at least once, and
find myself finally standing on the correct altar.  Holy crap!  I
double check my inventory to make sure I've got the Amulet, mill about
trying to think of anything to extract that last wish for (blessed tin
of Wizard of Yendor meat?), then say 'screw it' to avoid YASD from some
unforeseen circumstance - double-check the altar to make sure it's the
right one, then offer it.

I'd only made it to the Astral plane once before, like 5 years ago.  I
can't remember how I died, but I didn't play Nethack for awhile after
that.

